{"id":23,"course_id":9,"title":"Exec, Port Forward, and Probe Debugging","summary":"Use interactive commands carefully and know when they are diagnostic only.","sequence":4,"body_simplified":"## Interactive tools\n`kubectl exec` and `kubectl port-forward` are useful diagnostics, but they should not become the normal production operating model. Use them to confirm facts, then fix manifests, code, config, or automation.\n- Exec can check files, env, DNS lookup, and local process state inside a container.\n- Port-forward can test a Pod or Service without exposing it publicly.\n- Readiness probe failures usually mean no traffic should flow to that Pod.\n- Liveness probe failures should restart only when restart helps.\n\n## Commands to practice\n$ kubectl exec -n payments deploy/checkout -- printenv\n$ kubectl exec -n payments deploy/checkout -- nslookup payments-db\n$ kubectl port-forward -n payments svc/checkout 8080:80\n$ kubectl describe pod checkout-abc123 -n payments\nLook for probe path, port, initial delay, timeouts, DNS, config, and whether the app listens on the expected interface.\n\n## Safety note\nAvoid mutating files inside a running container as a \"fix.\" It will disappear on restart and bypass review.","body_traditional":"## Interactive tools\n`kubectl exec` and `kubectl port-forward` are useful diagnostics, but they should not become the normal production operating model. Use them to confirm facts, then fix manifests, code, config, or automation.\n- Exec can check files, env, DNS lookup, and local process state inside a container.\n- Port-forward can test a Pod or Service without exposing it publicly.\n- Readiness probe failures usually mean no traffic should flow to that Pod.\n- Liveness probe failures should restart only when restart helps.\n\n## Commands to practice\n$ kubectl exec -n payments deploy/checkout -- printenv\n$ kubectl exec -n payments deploy/checkout -- nslookup payments-db\n$ kubectl port-forward -n payments svc/checkout 8080:80\n$ kubectl describe pod checkout-abc123 -n payments\nLook for probe path, port, initial delay, timeouts, DNS, config, and whether the app listens on the expected interface.\n\n## Safety note\nAvoid mutating files inside a running container as a \"fix.\" It will disappear on restart and bypass review.","pinyin":"Scenario: Readiness probes fail after a port rename.\nChecklist:\n- Confirm the container port and probe port match.\n- Exec a local curl or wget only if the image has those tools.\n- Read app logs for bind address and startup errors.\n- Fix the manifest or chart values after confirming the mismatch.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl describe pod checkout-abc123 -n payments\n$ kubectl get deploy checkout -n payments -o yaml","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-kubectl-debugging-basics","course_category":"kubectl","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":86,"simplified":"kubectl exec","traditional":"kubectl exec","pinyin":"Interactive inspection","definition":"Runs a command inside a container for diagnosis."},{"id":87,"simplified":"Port forward","traditional":"Port forward","pinyin":"Local tunnel","definition":"Forwards a local port to a Pod or Service for temporary testing."},{"id":88,"simplified":"Probe timeout","traditional":"Probe timeout","pinyin":"Health setting","definition":"The time Kubernetes waits for a probe response before marking it failed."},{"id":89,"simplified":"Bind address","traditional":"Bind address","pinyin":"Network setting","definition":"The address and port an application listens on inside the container."},{"id":90,"simplified":"Diagnostic action","traditional":"Diagnostic action","pinyin":"Debugging","definition":"A temporary check used to confirm facts, not a durable fix."}],"flashcards":[{"id":52,"lesson_id":23,"prompt":"When should exec be used?","answer":"For diagnosis, such as checking env, DNS, files, or process state.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":53,"lesson_id":23,"prompt":"Why is editing a running container a bad fix?","answer":"It disappears on restart and bypasses code or manifest review.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":54,"lesson_id":23,"prompt":"What should you compare for probe failures?","answer":"Probe path, port, timeout, startup time, app bind address, and logs.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
